-- title:
--   The reverse endpoint of a cyclic k-step pair has displacement n minus k minus height
-- statement:
--   The reverse endpoint of a cyclic k-step pair has displacement n minus k minus height.
--
--   Let $n>0$ and write $I_n=\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$, represented by $0,\ldots,n-1$. The symbol $\operatorname{rep}$ takes a residue to its nonnegative representative and leaves ordinary integers unchanged. Residue sums and differences are cyclic; inequalities use representatives. $\mathfrak S(I_n)$ is the permutation group. Function symbols are written in curried notation; a subscript $\#$ means finite cardinality. $h(p,a)=|\{i:q(p,a)(i)<i\}|$ counts its ordinary descending arrows. $q(p,a)(i)=p(i)+a-p(a)$ is the anchored permutation, with arithmetic in the residue circle. $d_A(p,a)(i)=\operatorname{rep}(p(i)-i-(p(a)-a))-h(p,a)$ is the balanced anchored displacement.
--
--   The precise quantified assertion, with all its hypotheses, is
--
--   $$
--   \forall  (n : \mathbb N_{>0}) (p : \mathfrak S(I_n)) (a i : I_{n}) (k : \mathbb N), 0 < k \to  k < n \to  (q p a) ((q p a) i) = i \to  (q p a) i = i + \operatorname{rep}\,k \to  d_A p a ((q p a) i) = \operatorname{rep}\,n - \operatorname{rep}\,k - \operatorname{rep}\,(h p a)
--   $$
--
--   This supplies a local geometric constraint for the subsequent cycle and matching arguments.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/snow-ghost/glushkov-lx-lean/blob/8cddcd222b591673dc63475d9d45b9ed5c6eb92c/LX/OddUnitFixedNeighbors.lean#L16-L35

